-- title:
--   Transcendence of j(q^N) over ℚ
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a natural number, assumed nonzero. Inside the ring $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ of Laurent series over $\mathbb{Q}$ (realised as Hahn series with integer exponents), let `qExpand ℚ N` denote the ring homomorphism obtained by embedding the exponent domain along multiplication by $N$ on $\mathbb{Z}$, that is, the substitution $q \mapsto q^{N}$; it is well defined because multiplication by $N$ is injective and strictly monotone on $\mathbb{Z}$. Let `jqN N` be the image $\mathrm{qExpand}\,\mathbb{Q}\,N(\mathrm{jq})$ of the $q$-expansion `jq` of the modular invariant under this substitution, i.e. the Laurent series $j(q^{N})$. The theorem asserts that `jqN N` is transcendental over $\mathbb{Q}$ as an element of the $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra $\mathbb{Q}((q))$: no nonzero polynomial $p \in \mathbb{Q}[X]$ satisfies $p(j(q^{N})) = 0$. For $N = 1$ this is the transcendence of $j(q)$ itself.
--
--   This is the basic transcendence input for the construction of the function fields of the modular curves $X_0(N)$ as subfields of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated by $j(q)$ and $j(q^{N})$: it guarantees that $\mathbb{Q}(j(q^{N}))$ is a rational function field, so that degrees of the relevant field extensions are finite and computable. It is used in the computations of relative ranks and degrees attached to $X_0(N)$ and to the Hecke-type maps on it.
